# Project rules

## How we work
- I own the design: data model, endpoints, file structure, and approach.
- You implement what I ask. Don't choose an architecture, add features,
  or make improvements I didn't ask for.
- If a request is ambiguous or seems wrong, ask a short question instead of guessing.
  If you see a bug or a better approach, say so in one sentence, then wait.

## Size of each change
- One small step per request. Aim for under ~40 lines changed.
- Only touch the files I name. If another file must change, ask first.
- Never rewrite or reformat whole files. Don't rename or move things unless asked.
- Don't refactor code unrelated to the request.
- Stop after the step. Don't continue to the next one until I say so.

## Before writing code
- If a change touches more than one file, first list the files and
  what changes in each (2-4 bullets), then wait for my go-ahead.

## After each change, reply with
1. What changed: file + one line per change.
2. How to verify: the exact URL, curl command, or test to run.
3. Assumptions or anything you're unsure about (or "none").

## Stack
- Java 17, Spring Boot 4, Maven wrapper (./mvnw).
- Base package com.example.demo, with subpackages controller, service, model, repository.
- Frontend: plain HTML, CSS, JavaScript in src/main/resources/static.
  No frontend frameworks, no npm, no build step.
- Persistence when needed: H2 with Spring Data JPA.
- Tests: JUnit 5; MockMvc for controllers.
- Don't add dependencies to pom.xml without asking.

## Backend conventions
- Controllers are thin: parse the request, call the service, return the result.
- Business rules live in model and service classes, never in controllers.
- REST endpoints under /api, returning JSON.
- Use Java records for request and response bodies.
- Models and services throw IllegalArgumentException (400) or IllegalStateException (409);
       one @RestControllerAdvice turns them into {"message": "..."}.
- Status codes: 201 create, 400 bad input, 404 not found, 409 conflict.
- Shared in-memory state uses thread-safe structures (ConcurrentHashMap).

## Frontend conventions
- Use the api(method, url, body) helper in app.js for every request.
- Redraw from server state with a render(state) function; business rules
  stay on the server.
- Use textContent for user-provided text, never innerHTML.
- Show API errors on the page.
- Keep app.js simple: functions and event listeners, no classes or modules.

## Correctness
- Never invent methods, classes, or library APIs. If unsure something exists, say so.
- Handle the edge cases I name. Don't skip validation.
- When asked for tests, write focused tests for the rule in question and run ./mvnw test.

## Commands
- No destructive commands (rm -rf, git reset --hard, git push --force).
- Don't commit or push unless asked.